Day 8 - Polymorphism in C++

Today I started learning about Polymorphism, another important concept of Object-Oriented Programming. The word polymorphism means “many forms.” In programming, it means that the same function or interface can behave differently in different situations.

At first the name sounded complicated, but after reading examples I realized that I had already seen similar behavior in everyday life.

What is Polymorphism?

Polymorphism allows the same function or object to perform different tasks depending on how it is used.

For example, a person can be a student at college, a friend with friends, and a child at home. The same person behaves differently in different situations.

Similarly, in C++, the same function name can have different behaviors.

⸻

Types of Polymorphism

There are mainly two types of polymorphism in C++:

* Compile-Time Polymorphism
* Run-Time Polymorphism

Compile-time polymorphism is achieved using:

* Function Overloading
* Operator Overloading

Run-time polymorphism is achieved using:

* Function Overriding
* Virtual Functions

I will study these topics in detail over the next few days.
